package mop;

import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.security.PrivateKey;
import java.security.PublicKey;

import br.unb.cic.mop.eh.*;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateStore;
import br.unb.cic.mop.PredicateVerdict;
import br.unb.cic.mop.Property;

/**
 * KeyPairSpec
 *
 * A JavaMOP specification of the correct usage of the
 * java.security.KeyPair class.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/blob/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyPair.crysl
 */
KeyPairSpec(KeyPair keyPair) {

    // KeyPair.crysl:27 also states `generatedKeypair[this, _] after Con`, and this
    // file has no write for it. None is fabricated, and task 5.10 has now made the record
    // this pass fed it: the predicate is required by no rule of the whole oracle, so a
    // write here would produce something nothing can consume; its other producing site is
    // `KeyPairGeneratorSpec.mop` (task 4.14), which already carries a deliberate-omission
    // record for it; and a clause with no site has no row in `predicate_graph.csv` to
    // carry a record of its own, because that inventory is of sites and not of clauses.
    // The record therefore lives in the change's ledger and in
    // data/gh105/evidence/f3-PreparedKeyMaterial.md, which is where a clause without a
    // site can be written down at all (researcher decision, 2026-08-22).
    //
    // What this event gains is the rule's own REQUIRES, both of them:
    // `generatedPrivkey[privateKey]` and `generatedPubkey[publicKey]` (KeyPair.crysl:23-24,
    // ledger clauses #15 and #16). They are the constructor's clauses -- what a program
    // may hand to `new KeyPair(...)` -- and they are separate reports over separate
    // objects, so they carry a code each rather than being folded into one composite the
    // way `CipherSpec.i2` folds the three key-origin predicates behind one clause.
    //
    // The reads are in the body, not in `condition(...)`: a false guard compiles to
    // `if (!(guard)) return false;` ahead of the transition, so a key pair whose halves
    // the monitor never saw would drop the constructor out of the automaton and the
    // `getPublic` that follows would be accused of a wrong call sequence (INV-INS-133).
    // A failed REQUIRES is not a typestate failure.
    //
    // Measured before the reads were written -- the composition is real and the identity
    // survives it: `new KeyPair(kp.getPublic(), kp.getPrivate())` over a generated pair
    // runs, `getPublic()` and `getPrivate()` return the same object on every call, and the
    // constructor stores the halves without copying, so what `gpu`/`gpr` marked is what
    // this read asks about. Both halves are also produced by `KeyStoreSpec` for a private
    // key, which task 5.6 wired in the same commit. `KeyPairSpec-public-cipher.txt` and
    // `-private-cipher.txt` are the corpus traces that reach this event, and both take
    // their halves from `bind` lines, which the harness performs without dispatching -- so
    // both answer NOT_OBSERVED here, honestly: nothing the monitor saw produced them.
    //
    // Arity one on both, which is what the oracle writes: `generatedPrivkey` and
    // `generatedPubkey` are one-place in every rule that ensures them -- KeyPair itself,
    // KeyStore, and nothing else -- so there is no arity to move here.
    // `returning(KeyPair keyPair)` and not `returning(KeyPair kp)`: the generator keys a
    // parametric monitor on the event parameters whose NAMES are the specification's declared
    // parameters, so a `KeyPair` bound under any other name binds no monitored object at all.
    // The event then gets the parameterless map and runs on the root monitor and on every live
    // monitor of the specification -- the same broadcast task 9.3 closed at `PBEKeySpecSpec`,
    // reached here through a name rather than through a missing clause, which is why G-BIND
    // did not see it until task 9.11 extended it to compare the bound name.
    //
    // The binding was written at task 9.11 and it outlives the automaton shape that
    // exposed it, which is why it is worth keeping the measurement here. Unbound, a `c1`
    // is broadcast to every live monitor of the specification, and where it lands depends
    // on the state those monitors are in: under the mandatory constructor the `gpu`/`gpr`
    // of a generator-produced pair drive that pair's monitor to `fail`, `__RESET` returns
    // it to `start`, and a broadcast `c1` is accepted there; under the optional one those
    // reads leave the monitor accepting instead and the broadcast `c1` arrives where `c1`
    // is `fail`, which is how the conforming trace `KeyPairSpec-observed-halves.txt`
    // gained a KEYPAIR-ORDER-00 it had never drawn. Task 11.6 has since made the
    // constructor mandatory again, so the second half of that measurement is history --
    // but the binding is not a workaround for either shape. A constructor event is about
    // the pair it constructs, and binding the name is what says so.
    event c1 after(PublicKey publicKey, PrivateKey privateKey) returning(KeyPair keyPair):
      call(public KeyPair.new(PublicKey, PrivateKey))
      && args(publicKey, privateKey) {
        PredicateVerdict privOrigin = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY, privateKey);
        if (privOrigin == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-CONSTR-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a private key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='the private key given to the KeyPair constructor carries a key-origin predicate the rule does not admit'"));
        }
        else if (privOrigin == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-NOBS-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a private key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='no generator of the private key given to the KeyPair constructor was observed'"));
        }
        PredicateVerdict pubOrigin = PredicateStore.instance().validate(Property.GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY, publicKey);
        if (pubOrigin == PredicateVerdict.VIOLATED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-CONSTR-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a public key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='the public key given to the KeyPair constructor carries a key-origin predicate the rule does not admit'"));
        }
        else if (pubOrigin == PredicateVerdict.NOT_OBSERVED) {
            ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.UnsatisfiedConstraint, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
                "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-NOBS-01 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='a public key produced by one of the generators the rule names' msg='no generator of the public key given to the KeyPair constructor was observed'"));
        }
    }

    // `generatedPubkey[retPublicKey] after GetPubl` (KeyPair.crysl:28), read by `CipherSpec.i2`
    // since task 4.1 -- so this is one of the two sites of this pass that closes a chain
    // rather than recording a dead end.
    //
    // The write stays in the event body, against the default of INV-INS-134, and the
    // reason is recorded in `predicate_graph.csv`. Since task 11.6 restored the
    // constructor the rule makes mandatory (`KeyPair.crysl:20`), that placement is not a
    // preference any more -- it is what keeps this chain alive. A program almost never
    // constructs the `KeyPair` it reads from: it gets one out of
    // `KeyPairGenerator.generateKeyPair()`, a call this specification does not observe,
    // so `gpu` fires from the start state and the automaton rejects it. The event body
    // runs BEFORE the transition is decided, so the write happens anyway; a write at
    // `@match` would not, and `generatedPubkey` would go unwritten for every generated
    // pair in the corpus. That is not a hypothesis: the harness pair
    // `data/gh105/evidence/harness/f8g-*.md` shows the five traces that gain
    // KEYPAIR-ORDER-00 gaining nothing else, `SignatureSpec-generated-pubkey.txt` and
    // `-privkey.txt` among them -- their reads still answer SATISFIED, so the chain the
    // ordering report sits beside is intact.
    //
    // Task 7.1 is likewise closed: `order_alphabet_map_expert.csv` maps this
    // specification, so G-ORDER compares it rather than skipping it.
    event gpu after(KeyPair keyPair) returning(PublicKey publicKey):
      call(public PublicKey KeyPair.getPublic()) &&
      target(keyPair) {
        PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY, publicKey);
    }

    // `generatedPrivkey[retPrivateKey] after GetPriv` (KeyPair.crysl:29) -- and the seed wrote the
    // private key under `GENERATED_PUBLIC_KEY`, which is the defect task 6.1 names. It is
    // repaired here, with the write, and not recorded like the `preparedKeyMaterial`
    // conflation was, because the argument that kept that one recorded does not hold:
    // measured, renaming this write closes exactly the same chain it closed before, since
    // `CipherSpec.i2` reads the three key-origin predicates as one disjunction. What the
    // seed's name costs is downstream. Task 5.7 wires Signature's `generatedPrivkey[priv]`
    // and `generatedPubkey[pub]`, and it runs before 6.1: measured on the store, a private
    // key marked as public answers NOT_OBSERVED to `initSign(priv)` -- a report about a
    // conforming program -- and SATISFIED to `generatedPubkey`, which is a private key
    // answering the public key's clause. The read side of `GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY` already
    // exists; this is the write that was missing.
    //
    // Same body placement as `gpu`, for the same recorded reason.
    event gpr after(KeyPair keyPair) returning(PrivateKey privateKey):
      call(public PrivateKey KeyPair.getPrivate()) &&
      target(keyPair) {
        PredicateStore.instance().ensure(Property.GENERATED_PRIVATE_KEY, privateKey);
    }

    // The `KeyPair keyPair` field is gone with the accepting-state bookkeeping that was
    // the only thing reading it: the new store does not offer that bookkeeping and the set
    // never read it back (INV-INS-147). `@match` is left empty rather than deleted --
    // an `ere` names its accepting category whether or not a handler uses it, and the
    // write returns here at task 7.1.
    // The constructor is MANDATORY, which is what `KeyPair.crysl:20` orders --
    // `Con, (GetPubl | GetPriv)*` -- and what task 11.6 restored on the researcher's
    // decision of 2026-08-26, under the rule that governs the whole group: adherence to
    // the CrySL rule as the rule is written.
    //
    // The shape it replaces was `(c1 | epsilon) (gpu | gpr)*`, written at task 9.11 on two
    // grounds that have both gone. The first was the withdrawn generated rule, which
    // ordered `co?, (pu*, pr*)*`; the second was a project convention by which ORDER
    // answered to that catalogue while values answered to the expert one. D-16
    // (2026-08-25) ended the convention and left this file saying so in capitals, pointing
    // here.
    //
    // What the restoration costs is the 668-line measurement, and the measurement was
    // never wrong: practically every Android key pair comes out of `generateKeyPair()`,
    // which fires no `c1` at all, so every `getPublic()`/`getPrivate()` on a generated pair
    // is a sequence this automaton rejects. Measured again at 11.6 over the corpus as it
    // now stands: seven new KEYPAIR-ORDER-00 in five traces -- `KeyPairSpec-generated` (2),
    // `-observed-halves` (2), `-generated-cipher` (1), `SignatureSpec-generated-pubkey` (1)
    // and `-generated-privkey` (1). In a campaign over real apps the report will be common,
    // and the decision was taken with that on the table; the divergence row says it.
    //
    // What it does NOT cost is the predicate chain, and that is the fact the decision rests
    // on: the event body of `gpu`/`gpr` runs before the transition is decided, so both
    // halves are still marked on a pair the automaton rejects. The harness pair shows the
    // five traces gaining the ordering report and nothing else -- no NOBS anywhere, which
    // is what a broken chain would look like.
    //
    // The alternative that was weighed and refused: `creation event c1`, which JavaMOP
    // supports and `generic_new` uses in ten declarations. It would give a faithful ORDER
    // and no accusation at all, imitating CogniCrypt's seed semantics. But without a
    // monitor the event body does not run, and this file is the only producer of
    // `generatedPubkey`/`generatedPrivkey` in the set besides `KeyStoreSpec` -- the two
    // Signature traces and `-generated-cipher` would fall to NOBS. It trades seven ordering
    // reports for about four NOBS reports and for the loss of a real chain, which is worse
    // in both directions.
    ere: c1 (gpu | gpr)*

    @fail {
       ErrorCollector.instance().addError(new ErrorDescription(ErrorType.InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls, "KeyPairSpec", "" + __LOC,
           "v=1 code=KEYPAIR-ORDER-00 ev=" + __EVENTNAME + " obj=KeyPair val='' exp='' msg='the observed call sequence is not one KeyPairSpec accepts'"));
       __RESET;
    }

    @match {
    }


}